[Restrict Access to Control Pannel]



To create a new GPO:

right click on forrest (e.g aden.local)
>> 'Create a GPO in this domain, and Link it here...'
Name it as you please, the more standard the better. 

right click your GPO folder
>> Edit


User Config. >> Policies >> Administrative Templates >> Control Pannel >>
>> Prohibit access to Control Pannel and PC Settings (change to enabled to activate)

***Updated accurate version of understanding after I locked myself out DC from my own GPO*** (09/02/2026)
User Configuration → Policies → Administrative Templates
→ Control Panel
→ Prohibit access to Control Panel and PC Settings
→ Enabled

Purpose:
Prevents user accounts within the scope of the GPO from accessing
Control Panel and Windows Settings.

Important:
User Configuration policies follow the USER object, not simply the
computer being used.

Troubleshooting:
Use `gpresult /r` to determine which GPOs are currently being applied
to a user or computer.









[Disable USB Storage]


To create a new GPO:

right click on forrest (e.g aden.local)
>> 'Create a GPO in this domain, and Link it here...'
Name it as you please, the more standard the better. 

right click your GPO folder
>> Edit


Computer Config. >> Policies >> Admin. Templates >> System >>
>> Removable Storage Access >> Select and Enable 'All Removable Storage classes: Deny all access'
= doing so ensures people cant stick floppy discs, cds, flashdrives to corrupt your computer. 






** important note on linking, related to my previous lesson on locking myself out with a global gpo **

GPO Scope and Linking

A GPO is stored under Group Policy Objects, while the GPOs shown beneath domains or OUs are links to 
that policy. Linking a GPO at the domain root gives it a very broad scope, while linking it to a 
specific OU targets the users or computers within that OU. User Configuration policies apply based on 
the user's location in AD, while Computer Configuration policies apply based on the computer object's 
location. gpupdate /force forces policies to be reapplied immediately; it does not save changes. 
gpresult /r can then be used to verify which GPOs are actually being applied.



















